v23's outer region still uses the OLD arrangement:
```
	sltu	$2,$2,128
	beq	$2,$0,.L6        ; inverted, not likely!
	andi	$2,$3,127        ; delay: mask
	sb	$3,188($5)       ; then-arm inline
	j	.L5
	sh	$6,162($5)
.L6: ...
```

vs TARGET:
```
	sltiu $2,$2,0x80
	bnel $2,$0,L13644     ; likely, forward to single-remainder
	sb $a1,B              ; delay (likely)
	lw ...                ; else-inline starts with CURSOR RELOAD
	andi/sb masked ...
```

Differences: (a) branch form/direction; (b) in target, the ELSE arm comes INLINE right after the bnel — i.e., the outer-if's THEN arm was moved OUT of line. In mine, THEN is inline and ELSE at .L6.

For gcc to emit "bnel→then-remainder + else-inline", the source condition should be INVERTED TOO?! If source wrote:

```c
if ((u32)opcode_byte >= 0x80) {
    /* else-content INLINE */
    BC = opcode_byte & 0x7F;
    cursor reload...
    inner if...
} else {
    BC = opcode_byte;      /* this small arm goes LATE */
    A2 = wait_one;
}
```

Emission for if(cond>=){BIG}else{SMALL}: beq cond==0 → L_else(SMALL); BIG...; j end; SMALL; end. GCC might invert & relocate SMALL next to end: {bne cond→L_later? ...}. With cond=(op<0x80)=!cond: sltiu+bne-to-SMALL ✓✓!!! And SB(BC=raw b1) = SMALL-arm's first insn → hoisted into LIKELY DELAY SLOT ✓✓✓; remainder(sh one) after inner arms ✓!!!

SO THE OUTER CONDITION MUST ALSO BE WRITTEN INVERTED: `if ((u32)opcode_byte >= 0x80) { mask-path... } else { raw-store path }`!!

Combined source shape:

```c
if ((u32)opcode_byte < 0x80)  →  becomes else
```

FULL REWRITE:

```c
opcode_byte = *cursor;
if ((u32)opcode_byte >= 0x80) {
    arg0->field_BC = opcode_byte & 0x7F;
    cursor = arg0->field_38;
    arg0->field_38 = cursor + 1;
    opcode_byte = *cursor;
    if ((u32)opcode_byte >= 0x80) {
        high_bits = (opcode_byte & 0x7F) << 8;
        arg0->field_A2 = high_bits;
        extra_cursor = arg0->field_38;
        arg0->field_38 = extra_cursor + 1;
        arg0->field_A2 = *extra_cursor + (high_bits + 2);
    } else {
        arg0->field_A2 = opcode_byte + 2;
    }
} else {
    arg0->field_BC = opcode_byte;
    arg0->field_A2 = wait_one;
}
```

Hmm wait — but the INNER conditional in v23 I already inverted (>=). For consistency with emission both need inversion? Inner target emission: `sltiu(b2<80); bne→SHORT-later; third-inline`. Source `if(b2>=80){third}else{short}`: emission: compute cond2=(b2>=80)... gcc computes via sltiu(b2<80) + INVERSE branch: beq(b2<80)-false?? Let me just encode as: gcc emits `sltiu v0,(b2<0x80)` then branches `bne v0,0 → SHORT` — that's "if(b2<0x80) goto SHORT" which corresponds to source `if((u32)b2 < 0x80){short}else{third}` with arms SWAPPED by optimization OR source written as goto-style. Since v23 (source >=) PRODUCED exactly `sltu/bne→L8(short)/third-inline/short-last` ✓✓ THE INNER IS NOW CORRECT-ish (mine lacks the j in short arm because nothing follows it — once OUTER restructuring places single-sh AFTER, the j appears!!).

So: invert BOTH conditions. New concern: does `(u32)x >= 0x80` compile to sltiu(x<128) WITHOUT extra xori? gcc: (unsigned)x >= 128 ⟺ !(x<128): emits sltu $2,$2,128 into reg then uses BEQ/BNE inverted appropriately — no xori needed since branch can invert sense ✓.

